  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nMoist, cyclonic flow persists through tonight as an upper-level\nlow continues to weaken and depart to the northeast.\nConsequently, light high-elevation showers will persist through\nthe morning before flow becomes more northerly and advects drier\nair into the region. Most of the precipitation from these\nshowers will be in the form of snow given sub- or freezing \ntemperatures at the elevations in which they will occur. \nHowever, little in the way of additional accumulations are \nexpected given very light rates.\n\nA 1036 hPa surface high begins to slide eastward tomorrow night\nbeneath a building upper-level ridge. The dominance of these two\nfeatures will force dry conditions across the region through\nmuch of the upcoming week, though a weak disturbance passing by\nto the north on Thursday could challenge the longevity of the\ndry stretch in the Adirondacks. With atmospheric heights\nincreasing to 1-2 STDEVs above normal, temperatures will see a\nsubsequent above-normal moderation beginning Tuesday. Highs will\nreach the mid to upper 50s in valley areas with upper 40s to low\n50s across higher terrain from Tuesday onward through Saturday.",
